For his brief, as my choices within the signed plan, not his decisions. The five beats and their order are unchanged; nothing here needs his signature unless he wants to overrule it.

What I chose, in both the seventy and the full book ("The order after synthetic's read" in each outline):
1. A "Start here" page after chapter 1: the five steps as one printable checklist. Why: all three test readers reached the usable part too late; the ten already had "if you want to start now", and the checklist page was the picture they missed most.
2. The team pattern (one brief, one board, one hold) as a card in the first-rules chapter. Why: one reader wanted it there, and it is the method's frame.
3. The tour cut to four stops; the full tour moves to the full book's back matter. Why: all three skimmed it; he asked for the interface shown, and the four stops show it.
4. The rhythm chapter's counts into one card; the plumbing chapter one thing at a time, the register first. Why: the counts were skimmed, and the plumbing was where one reader felt it was for programmers.
5. Principles folded into Build your own, each step opening with its principle. Why: two of three read the principles as a recap.
6. The third factory said plainly as not yet run, first; a newsroom path beside the shop owner's in the last chapter.

Why not more: the readers are test instruments, three voices of one session, and the thirty, which kept the plain order, had three yeses. The seventy's order moves the practical part forward without moving a beat.

